WEBVTT

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:14.960
He warned us more than 25 years ago that human activity was changing the Earth's climate.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.410
Since then, the world has gotten hotter,

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:29.740
and NASA scientist James Hansen's warning has been echoed by the vast majority.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:32.110
of climate scientists everywhere.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:37.100
Global warming in the past century is about eight-tenths of a degree Celsius,

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.180
with most of it occurring in the last 30 years.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:44.800
And now, the warnings have become more urgent.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:49.500
I don't agree that we've passed the point where there's no hope.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.620
But on the other hand, we're darn close.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:59.980
Close because scientists have uncovered a new fact that may be...

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:06.950
masking the full impact of global warming called global dimming it's

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:12.830
powerful enough to alter temperatures in a matter of days it may have

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:18.040
contributed to the world's deadliest drought and it could mean that the

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:23.280
Earth's climate is about to start heating up as fast as the most dire

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:29.970
predictions I think we have less than a decade to avoid passing what I

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:37.140
call point of no return what will the future of our planet be now that we're dimming the sun

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:59.920
right now on nova future funding for nova

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.880
is provided by the following.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.860
September 12th, 2001.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.020
The aftermath of tragedy.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.150
Ironically, as America mourned, the weather all over the country was unusually clear and sunny.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:54.070
800 miles west of New York, in Madison, Wisconsin, climate scientist David Travis was on his way to work.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.880
of the day when I was driving to work and I noticed how bright blue and clear the sky was.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:07.440
And at first I didn't think about it, and then I realized the sky was unusually clear.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:18.960
For 15 years, Travis had been researching a relatively obscure topic, whether the vapor

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:23.310
trails left by aircraft were having a significant effect on the weather.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.930
In the aftermath of 9-11, the entire U.S. fleet was grounded.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:34.470
And Travis finally had a chance to find out.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:39.290
It was certainly, you know, one of the tiny positives that may have come out.

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:42.150
It was an opportunity to do research that hopefully will never happen again.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:50.390
Travis suspected the grounding might make a small but detectable change to the weather.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:56.080
But what he observed was both immediate and dramatic.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.020
We found that the change in temperature range during those three days was just over one degree C.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:13.830
And you have to realize that from a layman's perspective, that doesn't sound like much.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:16.700
But from a climate perspective, that is huge.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:26.770
The temperature range is the difference between the highest and the lowest temperatures in a 24-hour period.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:29.970
Usually, it stays much...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:46.660
the same from day to day, even if the weather changes. Not this time. Travis had come across

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:57.760
a new and powerful phenomenon, one which would call into question all our predictions about

00:04:57.760 --> 00:04:59.500
the future of our planet.

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:21.580
The trail that would lead to this extraordinary discovery began 40 years ago in Israel with

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:29.340
the work of Jerry Stanhill, a young English immigrant trained as a biologist.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:37.120
got a job helping to design irrigation systems. His task was to measure how strongly the sun

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:44.770
shone over Israel. It was important for this work to measure solar radiation because that

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:56.760
is a factor that basically determines how much water crops require. For a year, Jerry collected

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:58.960
data from a network of light meters.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.030
The results were much as expected and were used to help design the National Irrigation

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:05.410
System.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:20.220
Twenty years later, in the 1980s, Jerry decided to update his measurements.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:26.950
What he found stunned him.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:40.080
Well, I was amazed to find that there was a very serious reduction in sunlight,

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:42.200
the amount of sunlight in Israel.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:48.060
In fact, if we compare those very early measurements in the 1950s

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:49.620
with the current measurements,

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:54.900
there was a staggering 22% drop in the sunlight,

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.800
and that really amazed me.

00:06:58.130 --> 00:06:59.970
A 22% drop in...

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.950
in solar energy was simply massive.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:06.610
If it were true, surely the effects

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:08.490
would be obvious to every Israeli.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:11.500
The figures were hard for other scientists

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:15.260
to take seriously, so when Jerry published,

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:17.880
his results were ignored.

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:20.830
I must say that the publications

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:23.310
had almost no effect whatsoever

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:25.970
on the scientific community.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:36.480
But Jerry was not the only scientist who had noticed a decline in sunlight.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:46.580
In Germany, a young graduate student, Beate Liepert,

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:51.530
found that the same thing seemed to be happening over the Bavarian Alps as well.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:58.020
I was the same. I was as skeptical as any other climatologist.

00:07:58.220 --> 00:07:58.900
But then...

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:05.380
I saw the same results in Germany, so I believed him.

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:13.220
Germany, Israel, what about the rest of the world?

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:16.780
Working independently of each other,

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:19.980
Liepert and Stanhill began searching through journals

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:22.640
and meteorological records from around the world.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.970
Everywhere they looked, they found the same...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:44.970
Between the 1950s and the early 1990s, the level of solar energy reaching the Earth's surface had dropped.

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:57.100
9% in Antarctica, 10% in areas of the USA, by almost 30% in one region of Russia.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:03.960
and by 16% in parts of the British Isles.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:09.000
This seemed to be a global phenomenon,

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:11.940
so Jerry gave it a suitable name.

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:15.400
Global Dimming.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:25.250
But again, the response from other scientists

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:27.310
was one of disbelief.

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:29.970
The scientific...

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:38.490
community was obviously not ready to deal with the fact that there was a global dimming phenomena.

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:46.280
Jerry claimed that on average, the solar energy reaching Earth had fallen by two to four percent.

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:54.190
That should be making the world significantly cooler. Yet scientists knew the Earth was getting

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:59.760
hotter. As we burn coal, oil and gas, we increase.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.540
the concentration of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases in the atmosphere.

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:13.350
Like a thermal blanket, they prevent the sun's heat from radiating back into space,

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:16.210
causing global warming.

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:21.640
My friend's reaction, and actually to Charis and to my work at the same time, too,

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:23.990
was, oh my God, this is really extreme.

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:27.390
You are contradicting global warming.

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:29.970
Do you know how many billions of dollars...

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:36.540
spent on global warming research, and you and this old guy contradicting us.

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:43.070
So Liepert and Stanhill's work was widely dismissed.

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:50.950
Global dimming was not the only phenomenon that didn't seem to fit with global warming.

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:09.940
In Australia, two other biologists, Michael Roderick and Graham Farquhar, were intrigued by another paradoxical result.

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:15.810
The worldwide decline in something called the pan evaporation rate.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:20.720
It's called pan evaporation rate because it's evaporation rate for a pan.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:29.930
Every day, all over the world, people come out in the morning and see how much water they've got to add to a pan.

00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:33.830
to bring it back to the level it was at the same time the morning before.

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:34.650
It's that simple.

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:42.810
In some places, agricultural scientists have been performing this routine daily task

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:44.470
for more than 100 years.

00:11:46.030 --> 00:11:49.770
The long-term measurements of pound evaporation are what give it its real value.

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:55.550
And the fact that they're doing the same thing, day in, day out, with the same instrument.

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:57.690
Yeah, they deserve a medal, each of them.

00:11:57.890 --> 00:11:58.050
Yeah.

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:07.050
Nobody outside of agriculture took much notice of the pan evaporation measurements.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:12.970
But in the 1990s, scientists spotted something very strange.

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:17.290
The rate of evaporation was falling.

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:27.780
There is a paradox here about the fact that the pan evaporation rate is going down.

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:29.620
An apparent paradox.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:33.370
but the global temperature's going up.

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:40.710
This was a puzzle.

00:12:42.530 --> 00:12:46.110
Most scientists reasoned that like a pan on the stove,

00:12:46.450 --> 00:12:48.190
turning up the global temperature

00:12:48.190 --> 00:12:51.690
should increase the rate at which water evaporated.

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:56.140
But Roderick and Farquhar did some calculations

00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:59.970
and worked out that temperature was not the most...

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:07.500
important factor in pan evaporation well it turns out in fact that the key

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:13.350
things for pan evaporation are the sunlight the humidity and the wind but

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:19.700
really the sunlight is a really dominant term there they found that it

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:24.960
was the energy of the photons hitting the surface the actual sunlight that

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:29.580
kicks the water molecules out of the pan and into the atmosphere

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:37.690
And so they, too, reached an extraordinary conclusion.

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:43.680
You know, if the pan is going down, then maybe that's the sunlight going down.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:53.690
Was the falling pan evaporation, in fact, evidence of global dimming?

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:59.960
Somewhere in the journals, they felt, must be the hard numbers that could...

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:07.750
tie the two things together and then one day just by accident I had to go to the

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:25.440
library to get an article out of nature and as you do I couldn't find it and I

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:29.620
just glanced at it through the thing and there was an article called Evaporation

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:29.960
Losing

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:39.240
its strength, which reported a decline in pan evaporation over Russia, United States,

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:50.020
and Eastern Europe. And there, in the measurements, they said that the pans had on average evaporated

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:53.960
about 100 millimeters less of water in the last 30 years.

00:14:56.700 --> 00:14:59.980
Mike knew how much sunlight was needed to evaporate.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:06.310
a millimeter of water so he put the two sets of figures together the drop in

00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:12.420
evaporation with the drop in sunlight so you just do the same in your head 100

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:16.960
millimeters of water less of pan evaporation two and a half megajoules so

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:23.010
two and a half times a hundred is 250 megajoules and that was in fact what the

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:28.190
Russians had measured for the decline in sunlight in the last 30 years. It was quite amazing.

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:35.950
It was the same in Europe and the USA.

00:15:37.690 --> 00:15:42.100
The drop in evaporation rate matched the decline in sunlight

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:45.620
reported by Beati Liepert and Jerry Stanhill.

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:53.100
Two independent sets of observations led to the same conclusion.

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:59.540
Here at last was compelling evidence that global dimming...

00:16:00.090 --> 00:16:09.470
was real. All of a sudden you see, oh my god, the world is dimming and then all of a sudden you see,

00:16:09.850 --> 00:16:19.550
oh my god, this really has a tremendous impact. It had to be dimming in Europe, in America and in

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:24.210
Russia. This is on a global scale and we thought this is really important because the amount of

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:29.780
dimming was enormous, but this is big on a global scale.

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:37.310
What was causing it?

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:42.370
Scientists knew there was nothing wrong with the sun itself.

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:51.560
Culprit had to be somewhere here on Earth.

00:17:09.370 --> 00:17:15.660
The Maldives, a nation of a thousand tiny islands in the middle of the Indian Ocean.

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:29.880
It was here that Vera Badran Ramanathan, one of the world's leading climate scientists, began to unravel the mystery.

00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:32.560
of what's causing global dimming.

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:36.670
He had first noticed declining sunlight

00:17:36.670 --> 00:17:40.990
over large areas of the Pacific Ocean in the mid-1990s.

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:44.650
We didn't know at that time it was part

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:46.390
of a much larger global picture,

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:50.200
but I knew we had to find out what was causing that.

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:55.980
Ramanathan was certain of one thing,

00:17:57.260 --> 00:17:59.540
the big drop in sunlight reaching the ground.

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:04.060
had to be something to do with changes in the Earth's atmosphere.

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:09.400
There was one obvious suspect.

00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:17.840
Almost anything we do to create energy causes pollution.

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:25.640
Burning fuel doesn't just result in the invisible greenhouse gases,

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:27.450
which cause global warming.

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:29.950
It also produces visible...

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:35.020
tiny airborne particles of soot and other pollutants.

00:18:37.540 --> 00:18:40.720
These create the haze that shrouds many of our cities.

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:45.640
So Ramanathan wondered,

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:51.420
could this pollution be behind global dimming?

00:18:55.170 --> 00:18:58.640
The Maldives were the perfect place to find out.

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:12.240
The Maldives seem unpolluted, but in fact, the northern islands sit in a stream of dirty air descending from India.

00:19:15.050 --> 00:19:21.470
Only the southern tip of the Long Island chain enjoys clean air coming all the way from Antarctica.

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:28.880
So by comparing the northern islands with the southern ones,

00:19:29.540 --> 00:19:29.980
Ramane...

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:34.400
and his colleagues would be able to see exactly what difference the pollution

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:49.750
made to the atmosphere and the sunlight. Project INDO-X, as it was called, was a

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:59.890
huge multinational effort. For four years every possible technique was

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.900
to sample and monitor the atmosphere over the Maldives.

00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:18.080
INDO-X cost $25 million,

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:24.400
but it produced results, and they surprised everyone.

00:20:26.260 --> 00:20:29.980
The stunning part of the experiment was this pollutant layer...

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:31.580
which was about three kilometers thick,

00:20:32.510 --> 00:20:36.560
cut down the sunlight reaching the ocean by more than 10%.

00:20:41.290 --> 00:20:45.130
10% decline in sunlight meant that particle pollution

00:20:45.130 --> 00:20:47.070
was having a far bigger effect

00:20:47.070 --> 00:20:49.470
than anyone had thought possible.

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:56.830
Our models led us to believe the human impact

00:20:57.300 --> 00:20:59.960
on the dimming was close to half to 1%.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:03.880
So what we discovered was tenfold.

00:21:08.180 --> 00:21:11.100
Indueck showed that the particles of pollution

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:13.280
were blocking some sunlight themselves.

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:19.640
Even more significant was what they were doing to the clouds.

00:21:22.540 --> 00:21:25.540
They were turning them into giant mirrors.

00:21:38.510 --> 00:21:41.170
Clouds are made of droplets of water.

00:21:43.300 --> 00:21:46.540
These form when water vapor in the atmosphere

00:21:46.540 --> 00:21:48.920
starts to condense on the surface

00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:51.480
of naturally occurring airborne particles,

00:21:52.160 --> 00:21:54.340
typically pollen or sea salt.

00:21:58.920 --> 00:21:59.680
As they grow,

00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:04.990
The water droplets eventually become so heavy, they fall as rain.

00:22:23.720 --> 00:22:29.720
Ramanathan found that polluted air contained far more particles than the unpolluted air.

00:22:30.700 --> 00:22:34.380
particles of ash, soot, and sulfates.

00:22:37.010 --> 00:22:41.720
We saw 10 times more particles in the polluted air mass

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:47.000
north of the Maldives compared with what we saw south

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:49.440
of the Maldives, which was a pristine air mass.

00:22:52.760 --> 00:22:56.730
In the polluted air, billions of man-made particles

00:22:56.880 --> 00:22:59.780
provided 10 times as many sites.

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:02.640
around which water droplets could form.

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:07.900
So polluted clouds contained many more water droplets,

00:23:08.630 --> 00:23:11.920
each one far smaller than it would be naturally.

00:23:14.820 --> 00:23:18.300
Many small droplets reflect more light

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:20.050
than fewer big ones.

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:26.080
So the polluted clouds were reflecting more light

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:29.550
back into space, preventing the heat of the sun

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:29.910
from

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:38.980
getting through. This was the main cause of global dimming over the Indian Ocean.

00:23:43.930 --> 00:23:48.500
Basically, the global dimming we saw in the North Indian Ocean was contributed on the

00:23:48.500 --> 00:23:54.480
one hand by the particles themselves shielding the ocean from the sunlight. On the other

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:59.980
hand, making the clouds brighter. So this insidious...

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:06.980
consisting of soot, sulfates, nitrates, ash,

00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:10.630
and what harm you, was having a double whammy

00:24:10.630 --> 00:24:12.030
on the global dimming.

00:24:13.670 --> 00:24:15.550
And when he looked at satellite images,

00:24:16.450 --> 00:24:19.660
Ramanathan found the same thing was happening

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:28.870
all over the world, over India.

00:24:31.400 --> 00:24:34.660
over China and extending into the Pacific,

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:38.980
over Western Europe, extending into Africa,

00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:44.430
over the British Isles.

00:24:49.210 --> 00:24:53.710
But it was when scientists started to investigate the effects of global dimming

00:24:54.380 --> 00:24:57.540
that they made the most disturbing discovery of all.

00:25:02.650 --> 00:25:12.340
These more reflective clouds could alter the pattern of the world's rainfall with tragic consequences.

00:25:14.780 --> 00:25:19.880
Dawn, and as the sun breaks through the piercing chill of night on the plain outside Coram,

00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:22.060
it lights up a biblical famine.

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:29.500
Now, in the 20th century, this place, say workers here, is the closest thing to hell on earth.

00:25:37.260 --> 00:25:41.480
The 1984 Ethiopian famine shocked the world.

00:25:44.670 --> 00:25:49.560
It was partly caused by a decades-long drought right across sub-Saharan Africa,

00:25:50.630 --> 00:25:52.670
a region known as the Sahel.

00:25:57.760 --> 00:25:59.980
For year after year, the summer...

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:13.550
rains failed there were many factors at work but now there's evidence that among them was global

00:26:13.550 --> 00:26:29.920
dimming the Sahel's lifeblood has always

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:40.110
been a seasonal monsoon for most of the year it is completely dry but every summer the heat of the

00:26:40.110 --> 00:26:47.160
sun warms the oceans north of the equator this draws the rain belt that forms over the equator

00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:59.760
northwards bringing rain to the sahel but for 20 years in the 1970s and 80s the tropical rain belt

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:29.660
consistently failed to shift northwards and the African monsoon failed. For climate scientists like Leon Rothstein, the disappearance of the rains had long been a puzzle. He could see that pollution from Europe...

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:32.880
and North America blew right across the Atlantic.

00:27:34.060 --> 00:27:38.760
But all the climate models suggested it should have little effect on the monsoon.

00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:46.660
But then Rothstein decided to take the Maldive findings

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:49.980
about the impact of pollution on clouds into account.

00:27:51.230 --> 00:27:54.650
What we found in our model was that when we allowed the pollution

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:58.100
from Europe and North America to affect the properties of the clouds

00:27:58.100 --> 00:27:59.980
in the Northern Hemisphere, the clouds...

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:04.540
reflected more sunlight back to space and this cooled the oceans of the northern hemisphere and

